Practical No. 3
Aim:- Thread Creation & Synchronization – Develop a multithreaded program using the pthread library.

Theory:-
•	Thread:
A thread is the smallest unit of execution inside a process. Multiple threads of the same process share the same memory and resources.
•	Multithreading:
Multithreading means running two or more threads at the same time to perform tasks concurrently.
•	pthread Library:
The POSIX Threads (pthread) library is used in C on Linux/Ubuntu to create and manage threads.
•	Why Synchronization is Needed?
When multiple threads access the same variable simultaneously, incorrect results may occur. This problem is called a race condition.
Synchronization ensures that only one thread accesses the shared resource at a time.
A mutex (Mutual Exclusion) is commonly used for synchronization.

Code:-
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h> //creating and managing threads.

int counter = 0; //shared variable.

pthread_mutex_t lock; //Creates a mutex lock.

void *increment(void *arg) //void *function_name(void *arg)

{
    int i;

    for(i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

        counter++;
        printf("Thread %ld: Counter = %d\n",
               (long)arg, counter);

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }

    return NULL; //thread function has finished.
}
int main()
{
    pthread_t thread1, thread2;

    pthread_mutex_init(&lock, NULL); //initializes the mutex

    pthread_create(&thread1, NULL, increment, (void *)1);
    pthread_create(&thread2, NULL, increment, (void *)2);

    pthread_join(thread1, NULL); // wait until Thread 1 finishes.
    pthread_join(thread2, NULL); // waits for Thread 2 to finish.

    pthread_mutex_destroy(&lock);

    printf("Final Counter = %d\n", counter);

    return 0;
}
